<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ionian Greek, Ionian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ionia was the central part of the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, settled by Ionian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and home to cities such as Miletus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> in 546 BCE, the Ionian cities came under Achaemenid rule. Their later revolt helped spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionia.jpg" alt="Ionia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Athens2004 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
